-- #1036 LEG 2 — the module-visibility leg, and the regression the first cut of the
-- #1036 fix introduced.  This fixture exists to make that regression impossible to
-- reintroduce silently.
--
-- The dict word reaching a shared dispatcher is chosen PER MODULE: typecheck's
-- `keyForSiteByIface` upgrades a bare head tag to the canonical impl key only when the
-- SITE'S MODULE can see two impls at that head, because `KeyBuckets` is built from
-- that module's import closure.  The dispatcher, by contrast, is emitted ONCE for the
-- whole program.
--
-- Here `a` declares `impl Speak (Box Int)` and cannot see `b`, so a's site stamps the
-- BARE HEAD `"Box"` (hashName 193452654).  `b` declares the inheriting
-- `impl Speak (Box String)`; `entry` pulls it into the program but nobody ever
-- dispatches on `Box String`.
--
-- The first cut of the fix recomputed the collision verdict from a program-global decl
-- table, which COULD see b — so it emitted arms keyed only on the canonical keys
-- (2069167587134005782 and 850218145493699906).  a's bare-head word matched neither
-- and fell through to the interface default: `medaka check` green, eval `boxint`,
-- native `DEFAULT`, exit 0.  Silent wrongness, on a program that is CORRECT on main.
--
-- The fix is for the arm to accept EVERY word any module could stamp for the impl
-- (`implEntryRouteWords`) instead of betting on one.
--
-- ── THE AXIS THIS PINS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- IMPORT VISIBILITY.  Every other multi-module fixture in this corpus is a TREE in
-- which the module holding the dict-routed site imports everything that declares an
-- impl, so none of them can express "the site's module cannot see the sibling" — the
-- one axis leg 2 lives on.  Without this fixture a future RE-NARROWING of the route
-- word set back to a single recomputed verdict would regress silently with every gate
-- green, which is exactly how this class arrived.
--
-- Three variants, because their pre-fix symptoms differ:
--   Speak — the sibling in `b` INHERITS the default.  Pre-fix: `DEFAULT` (or SIGSEGV).
--   Loud  — the sibling in `b` OVERRIDES.             Pre-fix: `loudstr`, the WRONG
--           IMPL, i.e. silent wrongness with no default involved at all (#1064).
--   Sized — #1072, and the DIRECTION the first two cannot express: `a` holds the
--           strictly LESS specific impl, so the `min⊑` winner is the one `a` cannot
--           see.  Both engines got this wrong (native AND eval, every opt level) until
--           route resolution was scoped to the whole program rather than to `a`'s
--           topological prefix.  Encoding the prefix's answer canonically does NOT fix
--           it — measured: it keeps native wrong and makes eval wrong too, because the
--           bare head was what let eval's runtime re-resolve with full visibility.
--
-- Must print `boxint`, `loudint`, `boxspecific`.  Anything else means leg 2 or #1072
-- is back.
import a.{callA, callLoudA, callSizedA}
import b

main : <IO> Unit
main =
  let _ = putStrLn (callA ())
  let _ = putStrLn (callLoudA ())
  putStrLn (callSizedA ())
